{"text": "AGENDA ITEM NO.\n\nM-|\nCITY OF\n\ntine Fe OXNARD\n\nCALIFORNIA\n\nOffice of the Mayor\n300 West Third Street\nOxnard, CA 93030\n(805) 385-7430\nFax (805) 385-7595\nwww.ci.oxnard.ca.us\n\nJanuary 1, 2014\nDear members of the City Council:\n\nThe New Year brings great hope that the past year\u2019s sweeping changes made by this Council are\na down payment for regaining the trust of Oxnard's residents and for good government. Resident\ndemands for ethics reform, the elimination of the wide-spread fiscal abuses outlined in the\nVentura County District Attorney's Investigation Report, street and road repair and job creation\nhave been prioritized and acted upon by the city council. These efforts led to unusually long\ncouncil meetings in 2013 (next month the city policies and procedures committee will introduce\nproposals for shorter, yet more effective council meetings for city council approval), but the\ngroundwork for a more ethical, transparent and responsive government has been established.\nAs mayor, I strongly encourage the city council to accelerate efforts to regain and maintain the\npublic's trust in local government and to seize the following opportunities in 2014.\n\nFirst, the city council must hold firm to the goal of hiring a permanent city manager by March 1,\n2014. Most importantly, the city council should decide its role and the public's role in the hiring\nprocess. Should the city council wait until the consultant has narrowed the pool of candidates to\nthe finalists before interacting with qualified applicants or should that process begin sooner?\nWhen and how should the public best be incorporated into the process? Should a committee of\nlocal experts interview a pool of candidates and/or should finalists participate in a meet and greet\nsession with the public at large before a final decision is made? These important questions must\nbe addressed no later than the January 14, 2014 meeting to allow the consultant sufficient time to\nplan and reach a hiring goal of March 1, 2014.\n\nSecond, the process of hiring a new city attorney should also begin no later than January 14,\n2014, but not conclude before the city manager is hired March 1, 2014. The city council should\ndecide if the hiring process for the city attorney should mirror that of the city manager or\nconsider an alternative hiring process. Independent of this decision, the city council should set\nnew performance-based goals for minimizing lawsuits brought against the city.\n\nThird, city council members should incorporate the strategic priorities established in April, 2013\nwith a process for performance and priority-based budgeting. According to Alliance Formation\n(transformgov.org), \u201cpriority-based budgeting is a way for local governments to spend within\ntheir means by continuously focusing on the results most relevant to their communities...and to\nincrease citizen engagement.\u201d The city's finance director recommended last year that this\nprocess begin in October 2013, eight months before the start of the 2014-2015 fiscal year. To\nregain lost time, I recommend the city council aggressively pursue the following timeline and\nbenchmarks:",
